From: Mahdi Taziki <61482609+Mhd-tz@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winemac: Enter macOS full screen at start when WINEMAC_NATIVE_FULLSCREEN is set.

Some games' own fullscreen changes the display mode to one macOS doesn't actually apply on a scaled Retina display,
so the window is larger than the screen and the picture is cropped (Warcraft III 1.27 at 2056 x 1285 on a
1728 x 1117 desktop, 2026-11-01). The same games run correctly in a window put into macOS full screen with the green
window button, which keeps the desktop's resolution. With WINEMAC_NATIVE_FULLSCREEN=1 (set per launch by AnvilMac):
- the first window of the process that the green button can put into full screen (Wine gives it
  NSWindowCollectionBehaviorFullScreenPrimary: resizable, cycled, unparented, not maximized), at least 640 x 400,
  not already covering a screen and not mid-transition, is put into full screen exactly as that button does, once its
  frame has stayed the same for one second; once per process, so a player who leaves full screen stays out;
- in full screen the menu bar and Dock stay hidden (window:willUseFullScreenPresentationOptions: returns
  HideMenuBar | HideDock, and the app's presentation options are set so again on entering (and 0.5 s later) and
  after each activation, through an auto-hide step so AppKit sends them even when unchanged: the Dock otherwise came
  back at the bottom edge until the first click), so the edges, where strategy games scroll, reveal nothing;
- a frame the game sets itself while in or entering full screen (applying its resolution, with borders) is not
  applied, so the window keeps filling the screen; the game is told so, and the request is kept for leaving full screen. More than 5
  such requests a second are let through, so a game that insists can't loop;
- the pointer stays inside the visible part of the window (-[NSWindow setMouseConfinementRect:], as Wine's ClipCursor
  support uses; the window's frame within its screen, below the screen's top safe area, i.e. the strip beside the
  camera housing; not the content view's frame, which once gave 1720 x 962 of a 1728 x 1084 window). Set only when it changes, and again
  after an activation (which can drop it); a ClipCursor of the game's own takes precedence and ours returns after.
  (Warping the pointer back instead was tried: laggy at the edge, owner, 2026-11-02.)
- AppKit shows its arrow over the game's cursor on mouse presses without telling Wine (with the confinement it also
  announces the app's activation on every left press although it stays frontmost: 28 of 28 traced; right presses
  showed the arrow too, with no activation and the game re-sending its unchanged cursor). After every button press and
  release in such a window, and after each activation (then 0.1 s later and on the next mouse move), the client's
  cursor state is set again. (Keeping right presses from AppKit was tried and didn't help.)
Without the variable nothing changes. AppKit API (-[NSWindow toggleFullScreen:], NSWindowDelegate presentation
options) and the undocumented -[NSWindow setMouseConfinementRect:] Wine already relies on; no display-mode change,
no permission.

